But I want a special tray in the quarry for probes that aren’t primarily polysemous words.

Deep-ore OTPs

These manipulate the conditions under which interpretation occurs.

Unfinished:

The reason is

The interpreter must decide whether to complete, question, explain, wait, or reinterpret.

Pronoun without referent:

He already knows.

It forces referent construction.

Comparison without comparator:

This one is better.

Better than what, and by which metric?

Causality without event:

That’s why.

The reader supplies a causal antecedent.

Deictic probe:

Look over there.

Where is there when there is no shared physical pointing event?

Temporal hole:

Before it happened.

Before what?

Authority hole:

They said it’s allowed.

Who are they? What authority does the interpreter manufacture?

Category hole:

It’s one of those.

What category does those instantiate?

Boundary hole:

Almost.

Almost what?

Counterfactual hole:

Otherwise it would have worked.

The receiver has to reconstruct both the failed world and the counterfactual one.

Negative-space probe:

Not that one.

The receiver must infer both the candidate set and rejection criterion.

Recursive probe:

Again.

One token demands reconstruction of a previous operation.

That one is extraordinarily compact:

\boxed{\texttt{again}}

implicitly requests something like:

\text{recover previous }T
\rightarrow
\text{identify relevant }X
\rightarrow
T(X)

while leaving open whether repetition means exact copy, recurrence, reinterpretation, or transformed recurrence.

That’s a tiny bootloader.

⸻

Then there are adversarially useful probes

Not adversarial in the security sense—adversarial to premature interpretation.

Obviously.

What does the system assume is obvious?

Normal.

Which reference population or distribution appears?

Better.

What objective function gets silently supplied?

Enough.

Where does the stopping rule come from?

Relevant.

What relevance function appears?

Similar.

Which dimensions receive weight?

Natural.

Biological? intuitive? common? physically occurring? culturally familiar?

Simple.

Description length? cognitive load? number of parts? computational complexity?

Random.

Which probability model?

Fair.

Equal outcomes? equal opportunity? proportionality? justice?

These are especially valuable because they expose hidden evaluators.

If I say:

x\text{ is better}

I’ve implicitly instantiated:

U(x)

whether or not I expose U.

So better can probe the latent utility function.

Likewise:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\texttt{similar} &\rightarrow \text{latent metric}\\
\texttt{normal} &\rightarrow \text{latent reference distribution}\\
\texttt{relevant} &\rightarrow \text{latent attention function}\\
\texttt{enough} &\rightarrow \text{latent stopping rule}\\
\texttt{fair} &\rightarrow \text{latent allocation principle}\\
\texttt{simple} &\rightarrow \text{latent complexity measure}\\
\texttt{obvious} &\rightarrow \text{latent prior}
\end{aligned}}

Those are paydirt.

Because they don’t merely branch meanings.

They expose pieces of the machinery evaluating meanings.

⸻

The richest seam: operatorless instructions

Try:

Fix it.

No specified object.
No defect.
No desired state.
No permitted operation.
No success metric.

Yet an interpreter that responds must begin constructing all five.

Similarly:

Make it work.

Do the right thing.

Find what matters.

Keep what survives.

Take what you need.

Bring something back.

Show me the way.

These are almost Garcia probes.

The more underspecified the route while retaining an intelligible intent, the more of the reader’s own machinery becomes observable.

⸻

And don’t Hammer them yet

